Well, first you should use that scroll of enchant armor on your CoMR.
Every little bit of AC can help this early on.  For awhile after that,
you'll want to play conservatively.  Gain a few levels for the HP if
nothing else, and don't take too many risks.  Keep an eye out for
fountains and useless scrolls; you can blank them and write even more
?oEA.  (Be sure you don't overenchant anything though; if a piece of
armor is at +4 or higher, it's *not* safe to enchant.)

Both holy and unholy water will be useful to you.  If you get holy
water, bless the potion of gain ability and then drink it for a +1
to all stats.  If you get unholy water, first dip a stack of normal
water (which you can get by diluting other potions in fountains) into
the potion of unholy water in order to make more (Don't bother doing
this for the holy water this early; you can make it easier other
ways.)  Then curse the scroll of genocide.

When you think you can handle it, reverse genocide silver dragons a
chance at the armor.  Cold resistance will help with this; you should
also carve (or burn) Elbereth ahead of time all over the room, and
prepare a few chokepoints to help you deal with the 4-6 dragons you'll
get.  Perhaps others here can give you more specific advice on what
preparation you'll need for that; I usually don't get a chance to
reverse genocide until the mid-to-late game, so I've never fought
a group of dragons at low level.  But the armor is useful enough to
be worth some risk.  You might even consider trying with a gray or red
dragon first, since your cloak and =oFR will protect against their
respective breath weapons.  Their scales won't give reflection, but
the AC boost will be worth it.  You might even follow that up with
writing another scroll of genocide, cursing it, and fighting silver
dragons with your new AC. Use a scroll of enchant armor to turn
dragon scales (that you're wearing) into dragon mail armor.  Just be
*very* careful when trying this.  Early dragon mail can make your
game, but early dragons can destroy it just as easily.

The magic marker is definitely your best advantage here.  You can
write any scroll you've seen so far, and when you're high level
you'll have a good chance of writing scrolls and spellbooks you
haven't even seen yet.  The better the item, the more ink it will
use up, though.

I'm sure the others will have advice to add on top of this.  For
general advice, you might check out archmage's thread from May;
there are a lot of tips for beginning-intermediate players there.
